\fs28 \cf0 ##Earliest Origins (Prehistory \'96 Ancient Civilizations)
\fs24 \
\
Ancient Egypt: Both men and women wore wrap-style garments. For women, wrapped linen skirts or sheath-like wrapped dresses were common, fastened with belts or ties.\
\
Ancient Greece & Rome: The peplos and chiton involved wrapping fabric around the body and securing it with pins and belts.\
\
South & Southeast Asia: Long-standing wrap garments such as the sarong, lungi, and sari show the global presence of wrap skirts.\
\
Polynesia & Oceania: The pareo (or lava-lava) is another ancient wrap skirt variant.\
\

\fs28 ##19th Century
\fs24 \
\
Western women\'92s fashion became structured, with corsets and voluminous skirts supported by crinolines\
\
Wrap skirts appeared in practical garments, such as overskirts that wrapped around riding habits.\
regional costumes: Hungarian, Balkan, African and Asian traditional wraps continued\
not yet a mainstream fashion item but existed as a functional garment, particularly in rural and cultural dress traditions\
\

